#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

bool containsDuplicate(int* nums, int numsSize) {

    int size = numsSize * 2 + 1;
    int* hash = malloc(size * sizeof(int));
    bool* used = calloc(size, sizeof(bool));

    for(int i = 0; i < numsSize; i++) {

        int index = (nums[i] % size + size) % size;

        while(used[index]) {

            if(hash[index] == nums[i]) {
                free(hash);
                free(used);
                return true;
            }

            index = (index + 1) % size;
        }

        hash[index] = nums[i];
        used[index] = true;
    }

    free(hash);
    free(used);

    return false;
}

# 217. Contains Duplicate

## Problem

Given an integer array `nums`, return `true` if any value appears at least twice in the array.

Return `false` if every element in the array is distinct.

## Approach

This solution uses a **Hash Set / Hash Table** with **Linear Probing**.

Since C does not have a built-in HashSet, two arrays are used:

* `hash[]` stores the values.
* `used[]` tells whether a position in the hash table is occupied.

### Hash Function

```c
int index = (nums[i] % size + size) % size;
```

This converts the number into a valid array index.

The extra `+ size` handles negative numbers correctly.

### Collision Handling

Different values can produce the same hash index. When this happens, **linear probing** is used:

```c
index = (index + 1) % size;
```

The program checks the next position until it finds either:

* The same number → duplicate found.
* An empty position → insert the number.



## Complexity

* **Average Time Complexity:** `O(n)`
* **Worst-case Time Complexity:** `O(n²)`
* **Space Complexity:** `O(n)`

